## 1. General implementation

- Both asm.c and emu.c are written in C, targeting the ISO C89 standard.
- The code is structured with separate functions for parsing, label resolution,
  encoding, loading, and execution.
- Named constants and descriptive identifiers are used throughout.
- Source files include the required project and authorship information.
- The build uses the compiler flags specified in the assignment.

## 2. Assembler

- The assembler uses a single source-processing routine called twice for the
  two passes. Pass 1 records label addresses; pass 2 resolves operands and
  emits machine words.
- A symbol table detects duplicate labels and undefined references.
- Error checks cover instruction names, operand requirements, label syntax,
  numeric values, and operand ranges. Errors are reported with filename and
  line number.
- Decimal, hexadecimal, and octal numbers are supported, as well as signed
  operands.
- Object files contain 32-bit words, written little-endian, with the opcode
  in bits 7..0 and the operand in bits 31..8.
- Listing files show addresses, machine words, mnemonics, operands, and
  labels. The -dump option selects the plain memory-dump format.
- Instruction definitions are stored in a table (mnemonic, opcode, operand
  type), so adding mul and div required only two new table rows.
- Unused labels produce warnings without preventing successful assembly.
- The SET directive is implemented and demonstrated by test03.
- mul and div use opcodes 19 and 20.

The specification examples are covered by test01 through test04. Additional
valid and invalid inputs are covered by test05 through test16.

## 3. Bubble sort

- The bubble sort program is in test05.asm.
- It assembles successfully; the listing and log are included.
- The execution log shows the sorted result: 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 7, 8, 9.
- test09 adds a median test that sorts an array and selects the middle element.

## 4. Emulator

- The emulator loads binary object files produced by the assembler.
- Registers A, B, PC, and SP are implemented along with the full instruction
  set.
- Memory access bounds and PC range are checked; unsupported opcodes and
  invalid operations are reported as runtime errors.
- Options: memory dump before/after execution, instruction trace, ISA listing.
- mul performs 32-bit multiplication; div performs signed integer division.
- Division by zero is reported as a runtime error and exits with a non-zero
  status.
- An instruction limit stops programs that loop indefinitely.
- Invalid shift counts and out-of-range memory accesses are also caught.

Relevant tests: test04 (mul/div), test11 (division by zero), test14
(instruction limit).

